Chapter: OdeToJoy

 A SID music demo: Beethoven's "Ode to Joy" on C64 voice 1, a plain
 triangle lead. It is a standalone program -- its own opening, separate
 from the emulator's -- that drives the SID synthesizer directly to show
 the chip making music rather than a single beep. Build and hear it with:

   build/compile.ps1 -Src apps/c64/OdeToJoy.codex -Out ode.cdx -Log ode.log
   tools/codex-vm.exe -kernel ode.cdx -mem 3072

 Two parallel lists carry the tune: the note frequencies and each note's
 length in eighth-note units (a quarter is 2, a dotted quarter 3, a half
 4), so the two phrases land on their long notes instead of marching in
 equal steps. Each note holds gated for its length minus a short gap,
 then gates off for the gap so the next note re-plucks. sid-render-stream
 keeps the oscillator running across notes, so a pitch change does not
 click, and hda-play-pcm paces the whole thing to real time. The SID
 frequency register is Hz * 2^24 / 985248, so C4..G4 are 4455, 5001,
 5613, 5947, 6675.

  cites Foreword chapter List
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites C64 chapter Sid
  cites Kernel chapter HdaAudio

 We say:

Section: Playback

  unit-samples : Integer = 10000
  gap-samples : Integer = 1400

  gate-off-gap : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  gate-off-gap (regs) (rt) (buf) =
    let a4 = poke-byte regs 4 #10
    in let r = sid-render-stream regs rt buf gap-samples
    in hda-play-pcm buf (gap-samples * 4)

  play-note : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  play-note (regs) (rt) (buf) (freq) (dur) =
    let on = dur * unit-samples - gap-samples
    in let a0 = poke-byte regs 0 (bit-and freq 255)
    in let a1 = poke-byte regs 1 (bit-shru freq 8)
    in let a4 = poke-byte regs 4 #11
    in let r1 = sid-render-stream regs rt buf on
    in act
      p1 <- hda-play-pcm buf (on * 4)
      g <- gate-off-gap regs rt buf
      p1 + g
    end

  play-seq : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  play-seq (regs) (rt) (buf) (notes) (durs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length notes then 0
    else act
      p <- play-note regs rt buf (list-at notes i) (list-at durs i)
      rest <- play-seq regs rt buf notes durs (i + 1)
      p + rest
    end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Audio] Nothing = act
    let regs = alloc-bytes 32
    in let d5 = poke-byte regs 5 0
    in let d6 = poke-byte regs 6 #F0
    in let d24 = poke-byte regs 24 15
    in let rt = alloc-bytes 64
    in let z = sid-rt-reset rt
    in let buf = alloc-bytes (4 * unit-samples * 4)
    in let melody = [5613, 5613, 5947, 6675, 6675, 5947, 5613, 5001, 4455, 4455, 5001, 5613, 5613, 5001, 5001, 5613, 5613, 5947, 6675, 6675, 5947, 5613, 5001, 4455, 4455, 5001, 5613, 5001, 4455, 4455]
    in let durs = [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 3, 1, 4, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 3, 1, 4]
    in act
      print-line-uni "Ode to Joy"
      done <- play-seq regs rt buf melody durs 0
      print-line-uni "done"
    end
  end
